WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.263
This chapter covers hemodynamic monitoring and the diuretics that change it.

2
00:00:07.263 --> 00:00:15.628
We start with the central venous catheter, where it sits, what it measures and how to read it.

3
00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:24.438
Then the pulmonary artery catheter, its path, the wedge pressure and thermodilution cardiac output.

4
00:00:24.438 --> 00:00:38.410
Then the normal ranges, which differ slightly by source; the study guide names the chapter sixteen deck, so we use its values and recognize the RCP 105 ones.

5
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:44.728
We finish with the diuretic classes and their role in respiratory care.

6
00:00:44.728 --> 00:00:52.025
This chapter draws on the RCP 203 chapter sixteen deck, RCP 105, RCP 104 and Hess.

7
00:00:53.433 --> 00:01:05.556
A central venous catheter goes in through the subclavian or internal jugular vein, and its tip sits in the superior vena cava near the right atrium.

8
00:01:05.556 --> 00:01:11.208
The pressure there is right atrial pressure, commonly called the CVP.

9
00:01:11.208 --> 00:01:26.361
It measures right heart filling pressure, the right ventricular preload, and helps judge fluid status and right heart function, but it is often late to reflect changes on the left side.

10
00:01:26.361 --> 00:01:38.238
The line can also give central venous blood samples, medications and fluids; a true mixed venous sample comes from the pulmonary artery catheter.

11
00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:44.053
A low CVP suggests hypovolemia, and a high CVP suggests fluid overload.

12
00:01:44.053 --> 00:01:58.879
Venous return depends on mean arterial pressure minus CVP, so a higher CVP, from positive pressure ventilation or right ventricular failure, lowers venous return and cardiac output.

13
00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:04.858
Hess adds that a normal or high CVP correlates poorly with volume status.

14
00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:17.243
Level the transducer at the phlebostatic axis, the fourth intercostal space at the midaxillary line, and read at end-expiration.

15
00:02:17.243 --> 00:02:21.702
Do not take the patient off PEEP or CPAP to read it.

16
00:02:21.702 --> 00:02:33.278
If the PEEP is above ten centimeters of water, correct the measured pressure: subtract half of the PEEP divided by one point three six.

17
00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:41.167
Hess adds that adult catheters are usually seven French, sixteen to twenty centimeters long.

18
00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:49.089
The pulmonary artery catheter, the Swan-Ganz, is flow-directed and balloon-tipped.

19
00:02:49.089 --> 00:02:59.430
It measures the CVP, the pulmonary artery pressure, the wedge pressure, the cardiac output and the mixed venous oxygen saturation.

20
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:04.601
It is usually one hundred ten centimeters long with three lumens.

21
00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:16.055
The proximal port, about thirty centimeters from the tip, sits in the right atrium, and the distal port at the tip sits in the pulmonary artery.

22
00:03:16.055 --> 00:03:20.271
The balloon takes one and a half milliliters at most.

23
00:03:20.271 --> 00:03:28.703
Its path runs from the subclavian or internal jugular vein to the superior vena cava and the right atrium.

24
00:03:28.703 --> 00:03:39.760
Then the balloon is inflated, and the catheter floats through the right ventricle into the pulmonary artery and wedges in a smaller branch.

25
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.067
Then the balloon is deflated.

26
00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:59.929
The pulmonary artery pressure is read with the balloon deflated, and the chapter sixteen deck's normal is fifteen to thirty over four to twelve; RCP 105 gives fifteen to twenty five over six to twelve.

27
00:03:59.929 --> 00:04:09.266
The wedge pressure is read at end-expiration with the balloon inflated only momentarily, then deflated right away.

28
00:04:09.266 --> 00:04:14.426
It reflects left atrial pressure, the left ventricular preload.

29
00:04:14.426 --> 00:04:22.699
A wedge pressure above eighteen means cardiogenic pulmonary edema, while in ARDS it is normal or low.

30
00:04:22.699 --> 00:04:36.540
Thermodilution cardiac output puts ten milliliters of iced or room temperature fluid into the proximal port, and the thermistor at the tip senses the temperature change.

31
00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:42.929
Hess adds that pulmonary artery catheters have fallen out of favor in the ICU.

32
00:04:42.929 --> 00:04:50.792
They are now kept for left heart failure, pulmonary hypertension and cardiac output measurement.

33
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.621
The ranges differ by source.

34
00:04:54.621 --> 00:05:18.572
The study guide names the chapter sixteen deck, so give its numbers: right atrial pressure about two to six millimeters of mercury, right ventricle fifteen to thirty over zero to eight, pulmonary artery fifteen to thirty over four to twelve, and a wedge of about six to twelve.

35
00:05:18.572 --> 00:05:34.049
Recognize the RCP 105 slide values too: CVP zero to six, right atrial pressure two to seven, wedge eight to twelve, and pulmonary artery fifteen to twenty five over six to twelve.

36
00:05:34.049 --> 00:05:41.226
Cardiac output, stroke volume times heart rate, is four to eight liters per minute.

37
00:05:41.226 --> 00:05:53.072
Cardiac index, the cardiac output divided by body surface area, is two point five to three point five liters per minute per square meter.

38
00:05:53.072 --> 00:05:55.925
Hess agrees on the cardiac index.

39
00:05:57.333 --> 00:06:02.951
The study guide asks for PACP, but that is not a term any source uses.

40
00:06:02.951 --> 00:06:16.033
The decks say pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, also called pulmonary artery wedge pressure, and Hess says pulmonary artery occlusion pressure or wedge pressure.

41
00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:24.701
So PACP most likely means that wedge or occlusion pressure, about six to twelve on the chapter sixteen deck.

42
00:06:24.701 --> 00:06:33.208
If your instructor means the pulmonary artery pressure instead, use fifteen to thirty over four to twelve.

43
00:06:34.633 --> 00:06:35.921
Pause and think.

44
00:06:35.921 --> 00:06:41.797
A patient with acute pulmonary edema has a wedge pressure of twenty four.

45
00:06:41.797 --> 00:06:51.858
Is this more consistent with cardiogenic pulmonary edema or with ARDS, and how should that wedge pressure have been measured?

46
00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:58.767
[6-second practice pause]

47
00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:07.845
A wedge pressure above eighteen means cardiogenic pulmonary edema, and twenty four is well above that.

48
00:07:07.845 --> 00:07:11.545
In ARDS the wedge pressure is normal or low.

49
00:07:11.545 --> 00:07:23.992
It should be read at end-expiration, with the balloon inflated only momentarily and then deflated immediately, so the catheter does not stay wedged.

50
00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:36.348
All diuretics block sodium reabsorption; water follows salt, and the site in the nephron sets the strength and the electrolyte effects.

51
00:07:36.348 --> 00:07:44.782
The deck's memory trick: loops for lungs, thiazides for blood pressure, potassium-sparing for potassium.

52
00:07:44.782 --> 00:07:55.811
Loop diuretics act on the ascending loop of Henle and are the most potent: furosemide, Lasix, bumetanide, torsemide and ethacrynic acid.

53
00:07:55.811 --> 00:08:06.191
They are the drug of choice for pulmonary edema, and cause low potassium, metabolic alkalosis, volume depletion and ototoxicity.

54
00:08:06.191 --> 00:08:14.058
Thiazides act on the distal tubule: hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone and, in Hess, metolazone.

55
00:08:14.058 --> 00:08:25.654
They are first line for mild hypertension and chronic heart failure, not for acute pulmonary edema, and cause low potassium and raised glucose.

56
00:08:25.654 --> 00:08:37.900
Potassium-sparing diuretics, spironolactone, eplerenone, triamterene and amiloride, protect potassium, often alongside loops, and cause high potassium.

57
00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.110
The deck calls the last two classes low yield.

58
00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:49.487
Osmotic diuretics, such as mannitol, act on the proximal tubule and the loop.

59
00:08:49.487 --> 00:09:01.413
They are used for traumatic brain injury with cerebral edema and for acute renal failure, but they can worsen pulmonary edema and heart failure.

60
00:09:01.413 --> 00:09:08.121
Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, such as acetazolamide, act on the proximal tubule.

61
00:09:08.121 --> 00:09:18.225
They are used for glaucoma, metabolic alkalosis and altitude sickness, and their key adverse effect is metabolic acidosis.

62
00:09:19.633 --> 00:09:28.811
Diuretics raise urine output to reduce extracellular fluid, which lowers blood pressure or removes excess fluid.

63
00:09:28.811 --> 00:09:38.399
The respiratory link is a chain: less fluid, less pulmonary edema, better oxygenation, and a lower work of breathing.

64
00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:48.232
In cardiogenic pulmonary edema the goal is preload reduction, and loop diuretics such as furosemide are the cornerstone.

65
00:09:48.232 --> 00:10:02.572
Intravenous loop diuretics cause vasodilation within five minutes, lowering the wedge pressure, blood pressure and systemic vascular resistance even before urine output rises.

66
00:10:02.572 --> 00:10:17.896
In ARDS, a falling wedge pressure is linked to better survival, but diuretics are not the main treatment, and in preterm chronic lung disease, routine or sustained use is not recommended.

67
00:10:17.896 --> 00:10:24.533
Mannitol should not be given for diuresis, because it first raises plasma volume.

68
00:10:25.967 --> 00:10:34.778
On the ventilator, diuretics that do not spare potassium cause potassium loss and a metabolic alkalosis.

69
00:10:34.778 --> 00:10:42.743
For a diuretic-induced alkalosis, stop or reduce the diuretic and give chloride and potassium.

70
00:10:42.743 --> 00:10:51.470
The deck's top three adverse effects of diuretics are hypovolemia, hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis.

71
00:10:51.470 --> 00:11:00.367
Hess adds that diuretics mainly treat hypertension and the edema of heart failure or renal insufficiency.

72
00:11:01.767 --> 00:11:02.629
One more.

73
00:11:02.629 --> 00:11:10.969
A ventilated patient on furosemide develops a metabolic alkalosis with a low potassium.

74
00:11:10.969 --> 00:11:15.667
What caused it, and what does the deck recommend?

75
00:11:16.567 --> 00:11:22.567
[6-second practice pause]

76
00:11:23.067 --> 00:11:33.546
Furosemide is a loop diuretic, and diuretics that do not spare potassium cause potassium loss and a metabolic alkalosis.

77
00:11:33.546 --> 00:11:41.144
The deck's treatment is to stop or reduce the diuretic and give chloride and potassium.

78
00:11:41.144 --> 00:11:42.542
Treat the cause.

79
00:11:43.967 --> 00:11:46.319
Here is the chapter in brief.

80
00:11:46.319 --> 00:11:59.782
The CVP catheter tip sits in the superior vena cava and reads right heart preload; level it at the phlebostatic axis and read at end-expiration without removing PEEP.

81
00:11:59.782 --> 00:12:13.083
The pulmonary artery catheter floats through the right heart into a wedge position, and a wedge above eighteen means cardiogenic edema, while ARDS is normal or low.

82
00:12:13.083 --> 00:12:31.818
Use the chapter sixteen deck normals: right atrium about two to six, wedge about six to twelve, pulmonary artery fifteen to thirty over four to twelve, cardiac output four to eight, cardiac index two point five to three point five.

83
00:12:31.818 --> 00:12:39.767
And for diuretics: loops for lungs, thiazides for blood pressure, potassium-sparing for potassium.
